INT. VANTAGE ARENA - BACKSTAGE GAMING PIT - NIGHT

Humming. A hundred fans cooling a hundred machines. Somewhere beyond the curtain the crowd roars at nothing, at a screen, at a score.

JUNO KESSLER, 20s, headset around her neck, sits alone at Station 9 while the finals lobby loads, and behind her, in the half dark by the cable trunks, a figure in a gray hoodie stands very still with his face turned away, and she has not noticed him, and the monitors all glow the same dead blue.

The loading bar crawls. 98%. 98%. 98%.

JUNO
Nobody's supposed to be back here.

HOODIE GUY
(low)
I know.

The bar does not move. Her headset crackles at her collar, a whisper under the static, a voice that is not a teammate's.

JUNO
(to her mic)
Coach? Dev? You hear that?

Nothing. Then, very faintly, from somewhere behind the curtain, the crowd goes silent. All at once. Like a hand over a mouth.

Dev Aranda, 30s, the team coach, pushes through the curtain with a lanyard and a clipboard and stops. He looks at the hoodie. He looks at Juno. The lights above the pit flicker one row at a time, front to back, toward them.

DEV
Juno. Walk to me. Don't look at the screen.

JUNO
It's, it's on my screen, Dev.

On every monitor in the pit, in the lobby chat, one line types itself out letter by letter, slow as breathing.

THE TWINS (O.S.)
(whispering, in unison)
We found you.

Juno turns. At the far end of the pit, two identical kids in tournament jerseys stand shoulder to shoulder, too still, mouths barely moving, and neither of them is breathing.

HOODIE GUY
(turning at last)
That's not who you think they are.

DEV
(over him)
Get out get out get out

^JUNO
They're not on the roster

Every monitor cuts to black. The fans die. In the silence the only sound is something wet and slow dragging across the concrete behind the curtain, and then the curtain moves, and the lights come back all at once, white, blinding, and the pit is empty but for three headsets still warm on the desks, and a lobby chat that reads:

SUPER: "PLAYER 4 HAS JOINED."
